WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.900
Good morning Hank it's Tuesday.

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:05.260
So back in August of 2009, somebody started a tumblr called "OBAMA IS LITERALLY HITLER"

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.080
which satirized all the comparisons between Barack Obama and the leader of the third-reich.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.040
Hitler analogies have a long history.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.380
The phrase "Reductio Ad Hitlerum" was coined way back in 1951 to describe arguments

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:19.680
that rely on comparing other people's position to that of the Nazis or Hitler

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.700
And even before Hitler was literally the worst, other people were.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:26.640
Like one common uber villain was the pharaoh from the Hebrew bible's book of Exodus

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:33.780
During the American Civil war, Abolitionists sometimes called slave holders Pharaohs, and Southerners sometimes called Abraham Lincoln a modern-Pharaoh.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.480
But these days we have Hitler and the Nazis to compare to everyone and everything.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:41.140
On social media we read that Hillary Clinton is like Hitler,

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.080
or Donald Drumpf,

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.120
or Ted Cruz,

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.220
or Bernie Sanders,

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.960
Also an actual candidate for president compared the contemporary United States to Nazi Germany.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.160
All of this isn't just inaccurate, it's counter-productive;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.820
for one thing, hearing your preferred candidate compared to Hitler doesn't make you less likely to vote for them,

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:01.100
you're just repulsed by the analogy, not least because it implies that you are a Nazi.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:07.820
But for another, the analogy imagines that something is terribly, terribly wrong with the United States, like 1930s Germany wrong.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.880
And that's just not true. The U.S. economy has grown each of the last six years,

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.200
unemployment has dropped throughout the 2010s,

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:19.080
crime is down, 90% of Americans have health insurance, the highest rate since the 1970s.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.820
The United States is not in some mortal peril.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:26.260
Now of course the U.S. does have big problems-Americans have too much student loan debt, for instance.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:28.580
High income inequality is hindering growth.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:32.180
Our healthcare system is way too expensive and not particularly effective.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:38.020
We desperately need comprehensive immigration reform, and we still have a very high violent crime rate compared to other rich nations.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:44.200
But we've always had big problems, like when we talk about making America great again or reigniting the American dream,

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.600
when exactly was this Golden Age we're hearkening back to?

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.580
Was it 200 years ago when the vast majority of people couldn't vote because they weren't land-owning, white males?

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.980
Was it a hundred years ago when most women couldn't vote?

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:58.260
Fifty years ago when interracial marriage was still illegal in half the country?

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:02.880
Or was the American Golden Age in like 1985 when unemployment was higher than it is today,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.160
median household income was lower and you were twice as likely to be murdered?

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.500
Plus there was no Snapchat

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:17.520
I feel like this imagining of a past that never existed furthers the notion that the U.S. is facing some unprecedented danger that only candidate x or y can solve.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:25.500
And then when politics focuses on name-calling rather than policy, it becomes really hard to have a discussion because when you call my candidate an idiot or a loser or a cheat

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.660
you're calling into question my whole future.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:36.180
In a way it feels like you're calling me those things, which makes me want to remove myself from the conversation long before we get to the nuances of, say, immigration reform or tax policy.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:40.860
All the presidential candidates have done this sort of name-calling, but I don't want to create a false equivalence, here,

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.100
Donald Drumpf does it by far the most.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.120
Like here's just a sampling of Drumpf's insults:

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:51.880
Liar, choker, disloyal, a low-life crazy, dishonest, reckless, a hypocrite, and a soft, weak little baby.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.640
And that's just some of the things he's said about Ted Cruz,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:56.780
that I can repeat in a vlogbrothers video.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:02.920
Now putting aside the accuracy of those insults, I mean I quite like little babies but I've seen very little evidence to indicate that Ted Cruz is one.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:11.800
I think insults hurt American political life, which in turn makes our government less effective, regardless of where our political leaders sit on the ideological spectrum.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:18.600
And I really hope that eventually in this election cycle we can find ways to avoid insults and instead focus on what government should do and how.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.280
So Hank, you and I are gonna start trying to do that.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:23.580
Next Tuesday, I'm gonna take a look at the Republicans candidates' tax plans,

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.260
but first Hank, on Friday you're gonna take a look at the tax plans put forth by the Democratic candidates.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.960
I will see you then; here's to civil discourse.

